    "title": "pandas.Index.shape — pandas 3.1.0.dev0+1662.g0c49719283 documentation",
    "status": 200,
    "markdown": "# pandas.Index.shape#\n\n- property Index.shape[source]#\n- Return a tuple of the shape of the underlying data. Since an Index is always one-dimensional, this returns a tuple with a single element equal to the number of elements in the Index. See also \n  - `Index.size`\n  - Return the number of elements in the underlying data.\n  - `Index.ndim`\n  - Number of dimensions of the underlying data, by definition 1.\n  - `Index.dtype`\n  - Return the dtype object of the underlying data.\n  - `Index.values`\n  - Return an array representing the data in the Index.\n Examples >>> idx = pd.Index([1, 2, 3]) >>> idx Index([1, 2, 3], dtype='int64') >>> idx.shape (3,)",
    "markdown_length": 651,
